---
title: "stdlib-simhash-tutorial"
author: money-agent (AI-assisted, human-reviewed before submission)
date: 2026-11-28
abstract: A hands-on tour of stdlib/simhash.ltl: tokenising text, building 64-bit SimHash fingerprints on top of stdlib's canonical XXH64, and measuring near-duplicates with Hamming distance, with a runnable dedupe example and pinned test vectors checked line by line against the module source.

# stdlib.simhash: Near-Duplicate Detection in 64 Bits

Exact hashing answers "are these two texts identical?". Most real
questions are fuzzier: is this support ticket a re-worded copy of one
we already answered? Did this article get reposted with a new title?
`stdlib/simhash.ltl` implements SimHash, the classic locality-sensitive
fingerprint: similar texts land on similar 64-bit values, so "how
alike?" becomes "how many bits differ?".

Public functions from the module are called with the `simhash_`
prefix, the same convention the spiral test suites use for every
stdlib module (compare `aho_corasick_build` for
`stdlib/aho_corasick.ltl`).

## How a Fingerprint Is Built

`simhash_fingerprint(text)` works in three steps:

1. **Tokenise.** The text is lowercased and split on every
   non-alphanumeric character, so `"The Quick, brown-fox!"` becomes
   `["the", "quick", "brown", "fox"]`. Case and punctuation never
   reach the hash.
2. **Vote.** Each token is hashed with XXH64 (the module builds on
   `stdlib/xxhash.ltl`, whose output is canonical: XXH64 of the empty
   input is `0xEF46DB3751D8E999`, pinned in
   `tests/stdlib_spiral_wave_7.ltl`). For each of the 64 bit
   positions, a token votes +1 when its hash has the bit set and -1
   when it does not.
3. **Threshold.** A fingerprint bit is set when its accumulator ends
   at zero or above.

    let a = simhash_fingerprint("the quick brown fox")
    // a == 9021808664844435374

    simhash_fingerprint("The quick brown fox!") == a   // true

Because only the *sign* of each accumulator survives, repeating a
token scales every vote without flipping any sign:

    simhash_fingerprint("hello hello hello")
        == simhash_fingerprint("hello")                // true

Word order is ignored entirely — SimHash sees a bag of tokens, not a
sentence.

## Measuring Similarity

`simhash_hamming(a, b)` counts the bits in which two fingerprints
differ, from 0 (identical token multisets, up to sign) to 64:

    simhash_hamming(0, 18446744073709551615)   // 64
    simhash_hamming(10, 5)                     // 4  (1010 vs 0101)

`simhash_near_duplicate(a, b, threshold)` is the decision form:
true when the Hamming distance is at most `threshold`.
`simhash_similarity(a, b)` expresses the same idea as
`1.0 - hamming / 64`; the vectors pin its endpoints (1.0 for equal
fingerprints, 0.0 at distance 64). `simhash_fingerprint_all(texts)`
maps a whole list of texts to fingerprints in one call.

Adding a single token to a four-token text moves the fingerprint by
13 bits here — small edits move few bits, unrelated texts land far
apart:

    simhash_hamming(simhash_fingerprint("the quick brown fox"),
                    simhash_fingerprint("the quick brown fox jumps"))
    // 13

    simhash_hamming(simhash_fingerprint("the quick brown fox"),
                    simhash_fingerprint("quantum entanglement lecture notes"))
    // 24

## Two Gotchas, Pinned Rather Than Hidden

**The empty text fingerprints to all ones.** With no tokens, every
accumulator stays at 0, and the "zero or above" rule sets all 64
bits:

    simhash_fingerprint("") == 18446744073709551615   // 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

So the empty string is at distance `64 - popcount(x)` from any other
fingerprint `x`. Filter empty documents out before fingerprinting.

**The token hash call is one step removed from xxhash's public
shape.** Inside `simhash.ltl`, each token is hashed as
`xxhash_hash64(token)`, while `stdlib/xxhash.ltl` defines
`hash64(bytes, seed)` and `hash64_str(text, seed)`. The pinned
vectors use the only reading under which the module's own
spiral-wave assertions hold: the token's bytes hashed with seed 0.
Under that reading a single-token text fingerprints to exactly that
token's XXH64 — `simhash_fingerprint("a")` is `15154266338359012955`,
the canonical XXH64 of `"a"`.

## Runnable Example: a Dedupe Scan

The companion file `examples/simhash_tutorial.ltl` fingerprints four
short documents and prints the Hamming distance for every pair,
flagging pairs at distance 3 or less:

    doc 0 vs doc 1: hamming 0 -> near duplicate
    doc 0 vs doc 2: hamming 13
    doc 0 vs doc 3: hamming 24
    doc 1 vs doc 2: hamming 13
    doc 1 vs doc 3: hamming 24
    doc 2 vs doc 3: hamming 31
    1 near-duplicate pair(s) at threshold 3

Documents 0 and 1 differ only in case and punctuation, so the
tokeniser makes them the same text. Document 2 adds one word and
lands 13 bits away — above a strict dedupe threshold, which is the
point: pick the threshold for the job. Catching reposts tolerates a
wider distance than catching copy-paste duplicates.

## Pinned Test Vectors

The companion file `tests/simhash_tutorial_vectors.ltl` pins 24
checks in the spiral-suite style: the all-ones empty fingerprint,
single-token fingerprints equal to canonical XXH64 values, the
four-document fingerprint table with every pairwise distance,
tokeniser invariance under case, punctuation and repetition,
similarity endpoints, and near-duplicate decisions on both sides of
a threshold. Every constant was recomputed from the two module
sources, and the XXH64 mirror used for the recomputation was first
validated against the constant the repo itself pins for the empty
input. Run it the same way as any other suite in `tests/`.

## When to Reach for It

Use `stdlib.simhash` wherever "roughly the same text" is the real
question: dedupe queues, repost detection, clustering log lines or
tickets before a human reads them, cheap pre-filters ahead of an
expensive diff. It is a fingerprint, not a proof — confirm flagged
pairs with an exact comparison before deleting anything.

